<span class="kicker">2026-07-22 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Choose the Right Plate Compactor Size for a Patio or Driveway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>The right plate compactor size depends mainly on what you’re compacting and how deep the base needs to go. Driveways generally need a heavier compactor with a higher force rating than patios, since they carry vehicle weight and need denser, more stable compaction. Getting this wrong is one of the most common reasons DIY paver and base projects fail early, so it’s worth understanding before you rent or buy equipment.</p>
<p>At<a href="/about/"> Ridgewell Landscape and Design</a>, proper compaction is one of the most important, and most overlooked, steps in every driveway and patio we build across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and surrounding communities. Here’s what actually matters when choosing compactor size.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Question</b></td><td><b>Answer</b></td></tr><tr><td>Do driveways need a bigger compactor than patios?</td><td>Yes, heavier compactor, higher force rating</td></tr><tr><td>What matters most in choosing size?</td><td>Force rating, base depth, and material type</td></tr><tr><td>Biggest DIY risk?</td><td>Cracking pavers or uneven compaction</td></tr><tr><td>Best move if unsure?</td><td>Consider hiring a professional for base work</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><b>Why Compactor Size Matters More Than People Expect</b></h2>
<p>A plate compactor’s job is to press the base material, usually gravel or sand, into a dense, stable layer that won’t shift or settle later. If the compactor is too light, the base doesn’t get dense enough, which leads to sinking, shifting pavers, or cracking down the road. If it’s too aggressive for the material, it can actually damage pavers or create an uneven surface.</p>
<p>This is why “bigger is always better” isn’t quite right, the goal is matching the compactor’s force to the job, not just going as heavy as possible. Getting the<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> paver base depth</a> right is closely tied to this, since depth and compaction force need to work together for a base that actually holds.</p>

<h2>Turn Your dream landscape a reality</h2>

